{
  "title": "About Plan Plugin",
  "story": [
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "452360c0e34e1124",
      "text": "This plugin, type: plan, reads the state of plans off the plan pages themselves. A plan is a page whose title ends in Plan or Roadmap. On a plan page the item is that plan's status line; beside a plan, as the [[Plan Tool]] in the \u2630 menu, it reads the plan to its left; anywhere else it lists the site's plans newest edit first, each with its ticks done over total and its count of open questions, and each title opens the plan in the lineup."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "28d2927462811fe0",
      "text": "## Repositories\n\n- GitHub \u2014 https://github.com/Hitchhikers-Guide-to-the-Galaxy/wiki-plugin-plan\n- npm \u2014 https://www.npmjs.com/package/wiki-plugin-plan"
    },
    {
      "type": "plan",
      "id": "519eb697d1919efd",
      "text": "The five most recently edited plans on this site.\nLIMIT 5"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "2c9ed6f779d733f8",
      "text": "# Commands\n> One per line, in capitals; any other line is the caption."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "id": "aef99ed22f7f0ebc",
      "text": "CAPTION Plan item commands\n\n| Command | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| SUBJECT | the item is the Plan Tool: it reads the plan to its left, or lists the plans when nothing to the left is a plan |\n| SITE domain | list the plans of another site, read across origins from its sitemap and page JSON |\n| LIMIT n | show only the n most recently edited plans |\n| STATUS | the status line, said out loud: on a plan page it reads as a bare item does. It is the default text of a new plan item, so opening the item's editor and saving it unchanged never deletes it; an old item with empty text opens with STATUS already in its editor (since 0.5.0) |\n| SKILLS a, b | on a plan's status line: the skills Do Next Phase and Revise Plan give the model beside fedwiki-plan, read from ~/.claude/skills on the machine the farm runs on |"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "88d6d1b9a11c730b",
      "text": "A trailing colon is allowed (SITE: plan.localhost), the [[Fedwiki DSL Skill]] convention. A bare item, with neither SUBJECT nor SITE, is the status line when it sits on a plan page and the list anywhere else. A plan's status line carries STATUS from 0.5.0: wiki-client removes an item whose text is saved empty, so the empty status line of earlier plans was one unchanged save from deletion. Now deleting the text is a deliberate act, and the only way the item goes."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "2dd0621d9309a30f",
      "text": "# Three Faces\n> Decided by the item's text and the page it sits on."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "4741ccff87e2083d",
      "text": "The status face is a bare item on a plan page: it shows that page's own state, and one at the top of every plan is the plan's status line. The subject face is SUBJECT with a plan to the left: the item looks past ghost pages and tool pages (Selected Plugin Pages, Voice Tool, Plan Tool) to the nearest real page, and when that page is a plan it shows its state headed by its title. The list face is everything else: SITE, SUBJECT with no plan to the left, or a bare item on a page that is not a plan. Since 0.3.0 the state view folds: the summary line stays, with the next phase, the counts of blockers and lints, and a toggle at its head, and the phases, questions and lints beneath it are hidden until the toggle is clicked. The status line on a plan page starts folded, so it is one line plus its buttons; the Plan Tool's own page starts open. The choice is remembered per item for the session."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "374ddb2875ebf934",
      "text": "# The State View\n> Phases, the next phase, its blockers, questions and lints."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "0fc5c809cbe7175e",
      "text": "Phases are the plan's headings: each section from a heading to the next that holds at least one checkpoint, the Open Questions section excepted, shown with its ticks done over total. The next phase is the first with an unticked checkpoint; beneath it are its blockers, the unanswered questions and the red links inside its section. A red link is a link whose page is on neither the plan's site nor any site in the neighbourhood."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "4b93d3def7d0b6ab",
      "text": "Open Questions are the task lines of the Open Questions section that hold a link whose title ends in a question mark. A question is answered in one of two ways: a page exists behind its link, or the line itself carries the answer after the link, written `*Answer: \u2026*` (also `**Answer:**` or plain `Answer:`), as on the [https://dns.private.fish/view/resource-map-plan Resource Map Plan]. A reply on the line that ends in a question mark, such as *Answer: Explain more?*, asks back: the view lists it as asked back, it stays a blocker, and Revise Plan rewords that question instead of acting on it. The view says how many questions are answered and how, and links the rest; the tick on a question line is the author's own mark and is reported beside the count, never taken as the answer."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "a44f58860228fb51",
      "text": "Three house lints show only when they trigger: every checkpoint ticked but no link on the page whose title ends in Demo; a file named in a markdown item but not linked; and scatter, the same title on another site in the neighbourhood with no fork lineage between the copies, the rule of the Plan Index lint."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "5593cb46bbf7f70e",
      "text": "# What It Reads\n> Page JSON only, nothing stored."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "5c270a1b2c085917",
      "text": "The list comes from the site's sitemap: every title ending in the word Plan or Roadmap, skipping the Plan Index and Current Plan pages, the same rule the [[Plan Index]] board uses. Each plan's own JSON then gives three things."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "67e07e31a2d31e33",
      "text": "In the list, a plan's ticks are counted as the state view counts them: the checkpoints of its phases, the same done over total its status line and the Plan Tool show, so a plan reads one fraction in every face. The [[Plan Index]] board keeps its own rule and counts every task line on the page, Open Questions included, so the two can differ for one plan. Open questions are the unticked task lines in the section headed `# Open Questions`, which runs to the next heading, counted apart. The order is the latest dated journal entry, newest first, with the title as the tiebreak and the sitemap date as the fallback."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "513940fb7bade9fb",
      "text": "# Page Actions\n> The tool acts on the page to its left as well."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "e62bcb49b80a1398",
      "text": "Beside any page, not only a plan, the Plan Tool ends with a **Trash Page** button and a link to [[Recycled Pages]]. Trash Page asks once, then calls the server's own delete, which moves the page to the site's recycler; the page leaves the lineup and the Recycled Pages page (shipped by the recycler plugin) lists it, where opening it and forking it brings it back. It is offered only for a page that is really on this site: not a remote copy, a ghost, a browser-local draft, a recycled page or a revision view. It exists because the usual delete, forking the page's creation ghost, needs revision 0 to open from the first journal action, and a page whose journal starts with a fork (the New plugin's create from a twin, or an interlink seed) opens the remote copy from that action instead, so the fork button never deletes."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "9421e8b9fd9be369",
      "text": "# Update Plan\n> The mechanical write, through the farm API."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "537b317e1ae61ce3",
      "text": "On a plan's status line, and on the Plan Tool beside a plan, an Update Plan button calls the Plan API's one operation, `POST /system/api/plan/update.json` with the plan's slug, on the plan's own site. It ticks every unticked Open Questions line whose question now has a page, on this site or on a sibling site of the farm, with one journal edit for each item it ticks, and writes the page whole. Nothing else is touched: phase checkpoints stay the author's, and a ticked question with no page is left as it is. It then re-renders the plan's markdown to `assets/<slug>/<slug>.md`, a port of the fedwiki-plan skill's plan-md.py that renders every plan on plan.localhost byte for byte as the Python does, and removes the site's index files together so the site reindexes. The status line needs no write of its own: it is this item, and the button reads the page again and re-renders it."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "7bf3f444ac5b95d4",
      "text": "The write is gated by the farm, not by this plugin: the operation is declared as a write in the plugin's `api/openapi.json`, and the farm plugin refuses it with a 403 unless the site is private and the caller has the owner's rights, the gate the [[Voice Tool]] uses. The button shows only on the plan's own site to a viewer the wiki has not said is someone other than the owner, and a refusal comes back word for word in its answer line. The [[Plan API]] page, generated by the farm plugin from the declaration, documents the operation."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "4f78e5575a09defb",
      "text": "Around the write the operation holds the farm WIP marker, the protocol of `wiki-wip`: an atomic lock directory in `~/.wiki-hosting`, a random token, and AGENT-WIP.json written whole, so another session sees the edit in progress. If someone else holds the marker live it answers 409 naming the holder and writes nothing; a claim with no heartbeat for 30 minutes is taken over. Its answer lists the questions ticked, those still unanswered, whether the asset was rendered and the claim's times."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "4b1648a795e6fb22",
      "text": "# Do Next Phase and Revise Plan\n> The agentic writes, through the shared Claude door."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "f3c25edf2403956f",
      "text": "Beside Update Plan, on a plan's status line and on the Plan Tool beside a plan, are two more buttons under the same owner rule. Do Next Phase calls `POST /system/api/plan/next.json` with the plan's slug, and optionally a phase; Revise Plan calls `POST /system/api/plan/revise.json`. Both are declared as writes, so the farm's gate refuses them with a 403 off a private site or without the owner's rights, and both answer at once with a job. The tool polls `GET /system/api/plan/job.json?id=` every three seconds, showing the elapsed time, until the job is done or failed; a refusal or a failure comes back word for word."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "1b775a9d60f3a471",
      "text": "The run asks the Claude door, `@fortyfoxes/wiki-claude-door`, the module the [[Voice Tool]] and this plugin both import: the Claude API where the farm holds a key, otherwise the Claude Code CLI as whoever is logged in on the farm's machine, with every tool disallowed, a seven-minute limit and the farm's default model, claude-opus-5-5 unless CLAUDE_DOOR_MODEL says otherwise. It is given the plan rendered as markdown, for Do Next Phase the phase's heading and checkpoints marked out, the SKILL.md of fedwiki-plan and of each skill the status line names on a SKILLS line, and the pages the plan links on its site, the answers to its open questions first, up to 120 kB. A skill the farm's machine does not have is skipped with a note."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "f8025429eae60cf8",
      "text": "Do Next Phase asks for a fixed shape: a one-paragraph summary, one section per checkpoint saying what to do, how to verify it and whether the pages already show it done, and a closing Proposed Ticks section listing the checkpoints it judges done, word for word, or none. Revise Plan asks for the whole plan redrafted in the house format, every existing tick kept and only what the answers justify changed, with a closing What Changed section."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "ad94e9772526b935",
      "text": "The answer opens as a ghost page to the right, titled Plan Title Phase N Report or Plan Title Revision with the date, each paragraph, heading and bullet its own item, with a line saying who wrote it and a See link back to the plan. Nothing is written to the plan: proposed ticks stay on the report. Save and link it, the Voice Tool's mechanism, saves the ghost by its own fork flag as a page on the plan's site and adds one link item to the plan, journaled as an add: a report's right after its phase's last checkpoint, a revision's at the end of the See section."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "1934584492cab5a7",
      "text": "The run holds the farm WIP marker from the call until the model has answered or failed, with a note naming it, such as Plan Tool: Do Next Phase demo.localhost/next-demo-plan phase 2, and a heartbeat every minute, and releases it in every case; if someone else holds it live the call is refused with 409 naming them. Jobs live in the farm's long-lived handler process and are mirrored to status/farm-tmp/plan-jobs, so a job whose process has gone reads as failed rather than lost. [[Plan Next Demo]] shows a real run."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "5ccd129a683db5f3",
      "text": "# Red-link\n> The links that go nowhere, researched and written."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "53c500d4dbfdf66b",
      "text": "Red-link does what the house has always meant by red-linking a page: it finds the links on the plan that have no page behind them and writes their pages. A link is red when no page with its slug is on any site of the farm, or of the farms named in WIKI_EXTRA_FARMS, and it is not one of the installed plugins' own pages. Links are read from markdown, paragraph and html items, outside fenced blocks, and from timeline event labels. Question links, any title ending in a question mark, and everything under the # Open Questions heading are never written. A page behind a question marks it answered, and answering is a person's act, by hand or through the [[Voice Tool]]."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "b2f5d5d328424364",
      "text": "The button calls `POST /system/api/plan/redlink.json`, which starts a job and takes up to six red links a run, one at a time. For each, the Claude door researches the term on the web from its primary sources: the CLI is given WebSearch and WebFetch and no other tool, in its own memory scope. The page is written at once, under the name as its makers write it (fx, jless, ChatHackers). It opens with a line saying it is AI generated and researched on the web for this plan. Sections follow as the subject needs, with commands as terminal items and a Links section of the sources read; a See section links back to the plan. Each page is new, with one journaled create, and never written over a page that exists by then. The tool's line names the page being researched, then links every page written, with any that were skipped or failed. A stub Red-link wrote in 0.6.0 that nobody has touched since, one create in its journal, counts as still missing: it is researched and written over the stub through the journal, so the stub stays as rev0. A stub anyone has edited is a page and is left alone."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "a3dabb6caad39b7a",
      "text": "# Answer, Finish Plan and Check Plan Index\n> Phase 5: a question handed on, a plan closed, the board checked."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "6f3c1891f97d0548",
      "text": "On the status line and on the Plan Tool beside a plan, each unanswered question carries an **Answer** link. It leaves the question's title in the browser's sessionStorage under `audionote.waitingFor` and opens the [[Voice Tool]] to the right of the page the item sits on: the Plan Tool's own page, so the plan stays in the lineup, or the plan itself on its status line. The Voice Tool looks past tool pages, plan-tool among them, takes the plan as the page it speaks to, and chooses the question as the page it is waiting for and as the page title, then removes the key. Saving the spoken answer under that title turns the red link blue, and Update Plan ticks it."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "ac355b1f5fa4ef0a",
      "text": "**Finish Plan** appears beside Update Plan only when every phase checkpoint is ticked and the viewer may write. It opens a form in the tool for the conclusion and the demo page's title, prefilled with the demo an existing Conclusion names or the first linked title ending in Demo, and Write Conclusion calls `POST /system/api/plan/finish.json` with the slug, the conclusion and the demo. The operation is declared as a write, so the farm's gate answers 403 off a private site or without the owner's rights; the handler answers 400 unless every checkpoint is ticked, a conclusion is given and the demo page exists on this site or a sibling site of the farm. Under the farm WIP marker it writes a `# Conclusion` section immediately above See, below Assets: the conclusion one item per paragraph, then the line The demo is followed by the demo's link. It replaces the body of an existing Conclusion instead, adds the demo to the See list unless it is there, journals each item as an add or an edit, re-renders the markdown asset and resets the index. The tool shows the farm's answer word for word, then draws the new items on the plan in the lineup. A Conclusion's demo line satisfies the no-Demo lint whatever the demo's title ends in."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "fc079ef5dfec3d41",
      "text": "**Check Plan Index** ends the list face, on the Plan Tool and anywhere a list shows. It reads the site's [[Plan Index]] and Backlog Plans as the fedwiki-plan-index script writes them, a markdown line per plan on this site and a reference item per plan elsewhere, and compares each plan's count with the plan page by the board's own rule, every task line on the page, so a fraction that differs has really moved. The report lists the plans missing from the index, those on it that no longer exist, the counts that moved, from and to, and the plans newly complete; plans on other sites are counted, not compared. Nothing is written: the index stays the work of the laptop skill fedwiki-update-planning until its logic is ported."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "a067ca3813e8385f",
      "text": "# Revise Site\n> Phase 7: the answers change the whole wiki, with a pause before they do."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "893ef63e3c38518a",
      "text": "A research site keeps its questions on a page of their own, Open Questions, linked from the plan's See section. Since 0.2.0 the tool and every service read that page too: the state view counts its questions, Do Next Phase and Revise Plan put its answer pages first in what the door is given, and Update Plan ticks its answered lines, journaling the edit on that page, not on the plan."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "3d39719931da1738",
      "text": "**Revise Site** appears beside Revise Plan when the plan links an Open Questions page or has an answered question. It calls `POST /system/api/plan/revise-site.json` with the plan's slug, a job like the others: the door is given every answer page first, then every page on the site in sitemap order up to 120 kB, a page past the budget as its title and first paragraph only, and is asked for a Revision Proposal in four parts: the tone shift in two or three sentences; a table of pages, Page, Action and Reason, the action Keep, Amend, Rewrite or New; the pages it will not touch and why; and every name of a person, place or venue in the answers, each an unticked box. It opens as a ghost page titled Revision Proposal, the table a Table Plugin item, with two buttons in the tool: **Pause** saves it as a page and links it from the plan, nothing else written; **Go Ahead** saves it the same way, then runs it."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "8a01d1ff79d74cae",
      "text": "Go Ahead calls `POST /system/api/plan/apply-revision.json` with the saved proposal's slug. The farm reads the page back, so the boxes ticked on it are the ones that count, and asks the door for each Amend, Rewrite and New page in the table's order, one at a time, the plan last, the tool's line saying which page of how many. Each page comes back whole and is written whole, journaled as the wiki journals edits, each entry naming the proposal and the answer pages it drew on, so a page can be rolled back from its journal. Items that are not markdown, a table, a timeline, a diagram, the assets list, the plan's status line, are shown to the door by id and come back whole; none is ever dropped. The plan's markdown asset is re-rendered after the plan, and the proposal gains a line saying it was applied, so Go Ahead is offered once."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "6c0f38003040c3e7",
      "text": "Whatever the table says, Go Ahead never deletes a page and never writes an answer page, the Open Questions page, which stays the record of what was asked, a page forked from or credited to another site, or a page listed under Untouched; each refusal is named in the account. A page that would carry a name whose box is still unticked, and did not carry it before, is not written: name not confirmed. The run holds the farm WIP marker throughout as plan: revise site and the site's name. On a public farm both operations stay shut until the farm's operator lists plan.reviseSite and plan.applyRevision in FARM_API_PUBLIC_WRITES."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "3d73cc7bfe1f2bd9",
      "text": "# Next\n> Phase 6: ship it and show it."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "f80c178da6af3127",
      "text": "Phase 6 ships it: wiki-plugin-plan 0.2.0, with Revise Site, is on GitHub and npm and installed on the Pi5, where \u2630 shows the Plan Tool on every site. What remains is the public help page [[Plan Plugin]] on plugin.fedwiki.club, the Plan Tool Demo built with the fedwiki-demo skill, and the Conclusion on the [[Plan Tool Plan]]."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "a180150d09b7d386",
      "text": "# See\n\n- [[Plan Tool]]\n- [[Plan Tool Plan]]\n- [[Plan API]]\n- [[Plan Index]]\n- [[Fedwiki DSL Skill]]"
    }
  ],
